      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(acp): use PRAGMA page_count instead of SUM(length(...)) for O(1) ledger byte estimation",
        "body": "## Summary\n\nReplace the O(N) full-table-scan in `estimateSqliteLedgerBytes` with a `PRAGMA page_count × page_size` query that completes in constant time.\n\n## Problem\n\n`estimateSqliteLedgerBytes` runs `SUM(length(session_id) + length(session_key) + length(cwd) + 32)` across all rows in `acp_replay_sessions` and `acp_replay_events` on every event write. This causes performance degradation as the ledger grows.\n\n## Fix\n\nUse SQLite's `PRAGMA page_count` and `PRAGMA page_size` — both O(1) metadata queries — to estimate the database file size instead of scanning every row and computing string lengths.\n\n`PRAGMA page_count × page_size` returns a fast approximate file-size estimate that is safe for trim gating: the database file includes index and free-page overhead, so if the file size is under `maxSerializedBytes` the serialized data certainly is too.\n\nA 10,000-iteration guard is added to the byte-trim loops as a safety measure since page-based estimates converge more slowly than the old per-row computation (pages are not always reclaimed immediately after a single row deletion).\n\n## Testing\n\n- Existing event-ledger unit tests cover the serialized-byte trimming path\n- The in-memory ledger test (`maxSerializedBytes: 900` with a 5,000-char payload) exercises the same trim threshold logic\n\n## Issue\n\nFixes #100622\n\n---\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
